.TH CLOG_SET_FORMAT 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clog_set_format \- change the output format at runtime
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

void clog_set_format(clog logger, clog_format_t fmt);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Updates the output format of the shared backing store.  Because the
shared store holds the format, and not the struct of each logger, the
change is at once visible to every logger handle that writes to the
same file descriptor: the root logger and every derived logger.

There are three formats.

  CLOG_FMT_LOGFMT (0)
    The default.  The library writes every record as one logfmt line:
      ts=<ISO-8601-UTC> level=<L> proc=<name>(<pid>):<tname>(<tid>)
      src=<file>:<line> func=<fn> [fields] msg=<text>
    clog_error, clog_alert and clog_fatal append backtrace
    continuation lines, each indented by a tab, after the main record.
    In proc, <tname> and <tid> are the name and the ID of the thread
    that wrote the record; each thread reads them once, the first time
    that it logs, and ccol_set_thread_name(3) renames a thread and
    updates that name.

  CLOG_FMT_JSON (1)
    The library writes every record as one NDJSON object followed by
    a newline:
      {"ts":"...","level":"...","proc":"name(pid):tname(tid)",
       "src":"file:N","func":"...", [fields,] "msg":"..."}
    A structured field becomes a top-level JSON key.  For
    clog_error, clog_alert and clog_fatal, the library
    embeds the backtrace as a "bt" array of strings inside the same
    JSON object.  Before it embeds a string value (the message, and
    the key and the value of every field), it checks that value as
    UTF-8 and replaces a byte or a sequence of bytes that is not
    well-formed UTF-8, one invalid byte at a time, with the Unicode
    replacement character (U+FFFD).  So a message or a field value
    built from arbitrary or binary data can never make a record with
    invalid Unicode content.

  CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG (2)
    The RFC 5424 syslog format, offered only by a logger that sits on
    an fd (one from clog_open_fd or clog_open_fd_mp).  A call to
    clog_set_format() with CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG on a logger that sits on a
    file does nothing and reports nothing; the format stays as it
    was.

    The library writes each record as one line:
      <PRI>1 TIMESTAMP HOSTNAME APP-NAME PID MSGID [ccol ...] MSG\en

      PRI        = (facility * 8) + RFC 5424 severity (mapped from
                   clog_level_t: TRACE=7, DEBUG=7, INFO=6, WARN=4,
                   ERROR=3, ALERT=1, FATAL=0)
      TIMESTAMP  = ISO 8601 UTC (same format as logfmt/JSON)
      HOSTNAME   = the result of gethostname(2).  The library keeps
                   only the bytes that are RFC 5424 PRINTUSASCII
                   (0x21-0x7e) and drops every other byte, a space
                   and a control character included, without
                   truncating the value at that point.  It writes
                   "-" when this leaves no byte at all, or when
                   gethostname(2) itself fails.
      APP-NAME   = the name of the executable (the basename of
                   argv[0]), filtered to PRINTUSASCII in the same
                   way.  The library writes "-" when this leaves no
                   byte at all.
      PID        = getpid(2)
      MSGID      = level string ("INFO", "ERROR", etc.)
      [ccol ...]  = RFC 5424 SD-ELEMENT with SD-ID "ccol" carrying
                   proc="<name>(<pid>):<tname>(<tid>)",
                   src="<file>:<line>", func="<fn>", and any fields
                   that clog_set_field() attached
      MSG        = the formatted message text

    In MSG, and in every value of proc, src, func and a field, the
    library escapes a control character (a literal newline and a
    carriage return included) with a backslash, as \en, \er, \et or
    \exNN.  It writes a backslash itself as \e\e, so the escaped text
    reads back unambiguously: a real newline appears as \en, and the
    two characters '\e' and 'n' appear as \e\en.  logfmt uses the same
    scheme for its own values.  A value of proc, src, func or a field
    is an SD-PARAM-VALUE, which RFC 5424 requires to be UTF-8, so the
    library passes a well-formed UTF-8 sequence through unchanged and
    replaces each byte that is not part of one with U+FFFD, as the
    JSON format does.  MSG carries no byte order mark, so it is the
    MSG-ANY form of RFC 5424: a receiver must not assume that its
    bytes are UTF-8, and the library passes them through apart from
    the escapes above.  HOSTNAME and APP-NAME can hold no byte that
    needs this escape, because the filter above takes both down to
    PRINTUSASCII.  Together this guarantees that the content of a
    record can never split that record across several lines.

    clog_set_facility() controls the facility, which defaults to
    CLOG_SYSLOG_USER.  The library writes a backtrace (which
    clog_error, clog_alert and clog_fatal produce) as
    separate syslog messages, one for each frame, and each message
    carries the same PRI, TIMESTAMP and MSGID as the original record.
    The library escapes the text of a frame with a backslash in the
    same way as MSG.  Every frame shares the TIMESTAMP of the original
    record instead of carrying its own write time, which is what lets
    a reader match a record with its own backtrace frames.

    Connect the fd to a syslog daemon before you call
    clog_set_format(); on Linux that is usually a SOCK_DGRAM Unix
    socket at /dev/log.  Keep each message under 2 KiB, to stay inside
    the usual datagram limits of syslogd.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
logger  Logger handle.
fmt     CLOG_FMT_LOGFMT, CLOG_FMT_JSON, or CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Thread-safe: the mutex of the shared backing store protects it.
CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG on a logger that sits on a file does nothing and
reports nothing.
Asserts when logger is CLOG_INVALID, stale or already closed.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
